## Why This Is Asked

This question tests ability to design cost-aware prompt orchestration, a practical constraint in cloud analytics workflows.

## Key Concepts

- Token cost estimation
- Budget-constrained orchestration
- Module selection strategy
- Latency-awareness

## Code Example

```javascript
function estimateTokens(prompt, overhead = 20) {
  return prompt.split(/\s+/).length + overhead;
}

function selectModules(prompt, budget, candidates) {
  let total = 0;
  const chosen = [];
  for (const [name, cost] of candidates) {
    if (total + cost <= budget) {
      chosen.push(name);
      total += cost;
    }
  }
  return chosen;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you refine costs with real model pricing?
- How handle dynamic budgets or partial module failures?

## Why This Is Asked
The question probes practical prompt governance: how to prove provenance and detect tampering from model transformations in a multi-tenant setting. It tests attack awareness, deterministic auditing, and safe-guard design under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Watermarking robustness under paraphrase/translation
- Non-semantic embedding and tamper detection
- Audit logging for per-tenant prompts
- Edge-case handling and false positives

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal watermark prototype (JS)
function addWatermark(prompt, tag) {
  return prompt + " [WM:" + tag + "]";
}
function detectWatermark(text) {
  const m = text.match(/\[WM:([A-Za-z0-9._-]+)\]/);
  return m ? m[1] : null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate watermark robustness across languages/models?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic watermark policies?
